drm/esp32s31: snap the damage rectangle to the scale lattice before the PPA

The port tree is gitignored, so this is the whole modified file rather than a
diff. Copy it back over
linux-71-port/drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-lcd.c before building.
CUMULATIVE for this file: supersedes 0039, 0038, 0037.

WHY
A fullscreen SDL2 client renders 320x200 and the panel is 800x480, so the
driver PPA-scales by 38/16 into a 760x475 picture centred at +20+2. That
scale is exact and the full-frame path is correct.

The engine does NOT scale a sub-rectangle by the picture's ratio.
esp32s31_ppa_srm_ex() derives the factor from the rectangle it is handed,
as (out * 16) / src TRUNCATED to sixteenths (esp32s31_ppa_scale_factor,
PPA_SRM_SCAL_FRAG_MAX = 16). So a damage rectangle whose height is not a
whole number of lattice steps asks for a factor that is not the frame's, and
the engine writes that band at its own scale. The content inside lands at
the wrong offset while the correctly-scaled copy underneath survives,
because nothing damaged those rows.

Seen as Chocolate Doom painting the status-bar labels twice - AMMO, HEALTH,
ARMS and ARMOR each appearing at two vertical offsets - and as bands of a
previous frame persisting across screen changes.

The origin was already aligned down to the engine's 32-pixel macro block,
which happens to be a multiple of the lattice step here, so the ORIGIN was
right. The SIZE was not, and the size is what picks the factor.

WHY IT ONLY APPEARED WITH CHOCOLATE DOOM - corrected 2026-09-12.

The first explanation recorded here was wrong and is retracted. It said SDL
1.2 clients report no rectangle, so the driver never saw a partial one.
prboom's own counters refute that: 1273 MIT-SHM ShmPutImage requests in a
20 s run, 80 of them identical. prboom goes through exactly the same
row-hash damage path as SDL2 and has been sending partial rectangles all
along.

The real reason is the scale factor, and it is a much better answer because
it is arithmetic rather than a story. prboom renders 320x240 and the driver
logs "mode 320x240 scales to 640x480 at +80+0": q = 480 * 16 / 240 = 32,
gcd(32, 16) = 16, so step = 1 and EVERY rectangle maps exactly. Chocolate
Doom renders 320x200 into 760x475: q = 38, gcd(38, 16) = 2, step = 8, and
any rectangle not a multiple of 8 rows misses.

So the bug was never about which library a client used. It was about
whether the client's chosen resolution happens to scale by a whole power of
two.

It also came and went, because the adaptive engine picker sends some size
buckets to esp32s31_lcd_scale_rect_cpu(), which maps every OUTPUT row back
through the frame's ratio and is phase-correct by construction.

WHAT
In the damage loop in esp32s31_lcd_plane_update():

  q     = out * 16 / render          (sixteenths, exact by mode-set)
  step  = 16 / gcd(q, 16)            (8 for 320x200 -> 760x475)

  x1/y1 align DOWN to the largest multiple of step that is <= 32 (so the
  macro-block alignment is kept where the lattice allows), x2/y2 align UP to
  step and clamp to the framebuffer. render_w/render_h are multiples of step
  by construction, so the clamp stays exact.

Cost: at most 7 extra source rows and 7 columns per rectangle.

The xlite-SHM Damaged request does still report no rectangle, and xshim now
has an adaptive row scan for it. It is NOT what fixed anything here and it
has never fired in a measured run: neither prboom nor Chocolate Doom uses
that request. Do not cite it as a win until a client is found that does.
